butory.\X0A\\X0A\## Review of Systems\X0A\*   **General:** Denies fever, chills, weight loss.\X0A\*   **HEENT:** Denies headache, vision changes, hearing loss, nasal congestion, sore throat.\X0A\*   **Cardiovascular:** Denies palpitations, chest pain (other than described), edema.\X0A\*   **Respiratory:** Reports shortness of breath and pleuritic chest pain (as above). Denies cough, wheezing, sputum production.\X0A\*   **Gastrointestinal:** Denies nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain.\X0A\*   **Genitourinary:** Denies dysuria, frequency, urgency, hematuria.\X0A\*   **Neurological:** Denies headache, dizziness (except as related to exertion/SOB), weakness, numbness, tingling.\X0A\*   **Musculoskeletal:** Denies joint pain, muscle weakness.\X0A\*   **Psychiatric:** Denies anxiety, depression.\X0A\\X0A\## Physical Examination\X0A\*   **General:** Alert and oriented, appears anxious, in mild respiratory distress.\X0A\*   **Vitals:** HR 110 bpm, BP 110/70 mmHg, SpO2 92% on room air, Temp 37.0°C (98.6°F).\X0A\*   **HEENT:** Normocephalic, atraumatic. Mucous membranes moist. No JVD.\X0A\*   **Cardiovascular:** Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. No peripheral edema.\X0A\*   **Respiratory:** Tachypneic (RR 24). Auscultation reveals decreased breath sounds at the right lung base. No wheezes or crackles appreciated. Percussion reveals dullness to percussion at the right lung base.\X0A\*   **Abdomen:** Soft, non-tender, non-distended. Bowel sounds present in all four quadrants.\X0A\*   **Neurological:** Grossly intact. No focal deficits.\X0A\*   **Skin:** Warm, dry, no rashes or lesions.\X0A\\X0A\## Investigations\X0A\*   **Chest X-ray:** Ordered and pending. (Note: Based on the provided ground truth, the X-ray shows uncertain/borderline pleural effusion, but no pneumothorax, consolidation, mass, or fracture.)\X0A\\X0A\## Assessment\X0A\1.  Acute onset right-sided pleuritic chest pain and shortness of breath.\X0A\2.  Possible causes include pleural effusion, pleurisy, pulmonary embolism, musculoskeletal pain, pericarditis. Given the decreased breath sounds and dullness to percussion at the right base, pleural effusion is high on the differential.\X0A\\X0A\## Plan\X0A\1.  Obtain chest X-ray (already ordered). Review results upon availability.\X0A\2.  Administer supplemental oxygen via nasal cannula to maintain SpO2 > 94%.\X0A\3.  Obtain EKG to rule out cardiac etiology (e.g., pericarditis, ischemia).\X0A\4.  Obtain CBC, CMP, D-dimer, BNP, troponin.\X0A\5.  Consider CT angiography of the chest if pulmonary embolism is suspected based on clinical presentation and D-dimer results.\X0A\6.  Consider thoracentesis if significant pleural effusion is confirmed on imaging and etiology is unclear.\X0A\7.  Continuous cardiac monitoring.\X0A\8.  Pain management with NSAIDs or acetaminophen.\X0A\9.  Admit to observation unit or inpatient ward pending further workup and management.\X0A\